### What is collected

**This website.** The site is static and does not require an account. If you
choose to use the <%= link_to 'contact page', '/contact' %>, the name, email
address, and message you submit are sent so that your message can be read and
answered. The site's host (Cloudflare) processes standard server request data
(such as IP address and browser type) to serve pages and protect against abuse.

**Guessr (the daily game).** Guessr has no accounts, no login, and never asks
for your name or email address. To keep score across the five rounds of a day
and put you on the leaderboard, your browser makes up two things the first time
you play: a random identifier that means nothing outside the game, and a
two-word alias chosen for you from a fixed list (you can reroll it, but you
cannot type your own). Both are stored on your device, described under "Cookies
and device storage" below.

When you submit a guess, what is recorded is the date, that random identifier,
the alias, which clip you were shown, how far your guess was from the answer,
and the points it earned. Your IP address is deliberately **not** stored
alongside it. Practice rounds are not recorded at all.

Your alias and score are public: they appear on the game's leaderboard, and they
may also be shown on screen during the live stream on Twitch and YouTube. Since
the alias is assigned rather than typed, it is not meant to identify you — if
you would rather not appear, don't play the daily game, or ask for your rows to
be removed using the contact details below.

The game offers a link (and a QR code) for playing on a second device. Opening
it moves that browser's scores under a single identifier, and only after you
confirm a prompt. Anyone you show that code to can join their scores to yours,
so treat it as personal to you.

**Tripbot (Twitch & YouTube live chat).** When you type in the live chat of the
A Dana Life stream, Tripbot reads public chat messages so it can respond to
commands (for example `!location`, `!weather`, or `!guess`). The only
information it sees is what the platform makes public: your chat **display
name** and the **text of the message** you send. Tripbot does **not** access
your email address, private messages, watch history, subscriptions, or any
data that isn't part of the public live chat.

On both Twitch and YouTube, your public display name and message text are
written to short-lived operational logs (used for debugging and abuse
prevention) that are automatically deleted within 14 days. On Twitch, your
public display name may also be associated with stream features such as the
miles leaderboard.

### How YouTube data is used

Tripbot uses **YouTube API Services** to read the live chat of the active
broadcast and to post replies back into that same chat. By interacting with
Tripbot on YouTube, you are also agreeing to the
[YouTube Terms of Service](https://www.youtube.com/t/terms). Information handled
through the YouTube API is governed by the
[Google Privacy Policy](https://policies.google.com/privacy).

Tripbot's access to the YouTube channel is authorized by the channel owner, not
by individual viewers, so there is no per-viewer login to revoke. The channel
owner can revoke Tripbot's access at any time through the
[Google security settings](https://security.google.com/settings/security/permissions).

Public chat data read through the API is used to generate a real-time reply. Your
display name and message text are also written to short-lived operational logs
(for debugging and abuse prevention) that are automatically deleted within 14
days, and are mirrored in real time to a private operator console so the stream
operator can watch chat as it happens. This data is **not** profiled, combined
with data from other sources, sold, or used for advertising. Apart from posting
Tripbot's reply back to YouTube's own API, it is handled only by the
infrastructure services that run these functions for us — a logging provider, an
error-monitoring service, and, if you send the operator a report, the alerting
channel that report is delivered to. A Dana Life, LLC is operated by one person
and shares this data with no one else.

### What is not done

- Your information is **not sold** or rented to anyone.
- Your data is **not used for advertising** or ad targeting — see "Affiliate
  links" below for the one form of advertising the site does contain.
- No attempt is made to identify viewers beyond their public chat display name.

### Affiliate links

Some blog posts contain Amazon affiliate links, marked with `#ad`. If you buy
something through one, A Dana Life, LLC may earn a small commission at no extra
cost to you. These are ordinary web links: they don't use your personal
information, and no viewer data is involved in showing them. The YouTube and
Twitch Tripbot chat experience itself contains no ads or affiliate links.

### Data retention

Contact-form messages are kept only as long as needed to respond to them.
Public live-chat data (your display name and message text, from both Twitch and
YouTube) is written to operational logs that are automatically deleted within
14 days. Some of this activity may also appear in supporting operational
records — error-monitoring diagnostics, and any report you choose to send the
operator — which are retained per those services' standard retention and used
only to run and debug the stream. Twitch chat activity that powers stream
features (such as the leaderboard) is kept for as long as those features run,
and can be removed on request.

Guessr scores (the date, the random identifier, the alias, the clip, the
distance and the points) are kept for as long as the game and its leaderboard
run, since they are what the leaderboard is. Clearing them from your own browser
is the "start fresh" button in the game; removing the recorded scores as well is
a request to the contact address below, and the identifier is the only thing
that ties them together.

### Cookies and device storage

**This website.** The site does not use analytics, advertising, or tracking
cookies. The only first-party storage it uses is functional: a `theme` value
saved in your browser's `localStorage` to remember your light/dark-mode choice,
plus the strictly-necessary cookies our host (Cloudflare) may set (such as
`__cf_bm`) to serve pages and protect against abuse, described in the
[Cloudflare cookie policy](https://www.cloudflare.com/cookie-policy/).

Some blog posts embed third-party players — YouTube videos and the Twitch
stream. When a page containing one of these embeds loads, that provider may set
its own cookies on your device, governed by its own privacy policy (see
"Third-party services" below). Pages without an embed load none of these.

**Guessr.** The game sets no cookies. It uses your browser's `localStorage` for
the state a game needs to work: the random player identifier and assigned alias
described above, which day you have finished and what you scored, a history of
your past guesses (kept for the "all my guesses" map, capped at the most recent
250), your light/dark-mode choice, and two flags recording that you have already
seen the intro and the about panel. All of it stays on your device — the history
map is drawn locally and never sent anywhere. The "start fresh" button in the
game clears everything Guessr has stored in that browser, which also means the
next guess you make is recorded as a new player.

Guessr uses **Cloudflare Web Analytics** to count page views. It sets no
cookies, stores no IP addresses, and does not follow you between sites; see the
[Cloudflare privacy policy](https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/).

The game's map is drawn from **OpenStreetMap** tiles, the end-of-game screen
embeds the **YouTube** live stream, and one small library is loaded from the
**unpkg** CDN. Loading any of these tells that provider your IP address and
browser, as any web request does; the YouTube embed uses the no-cookie player
host. See "Third-party services" below.

**Tripbot (YouTube live chat).** Tripbot runs server-side against the YouTube
API. It does **not** place, access, or recognize any cookies, identifiers, or
other storage on your device or browser.

### Third-party services

Using this site and stream necessarily involves a few service providers, each
with its own privacy policy:

- **Twitch** — [privacy policy](https://www.twitch.tv/p/legal/privacy-notice/)
- **YouTube / Google** — [privacy policy](https://policies.google.com/privacy)
- **Cloudflare** (website hosting, Guessr hosting and analytics) — [privacy policy](https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/)
- **OpenStreetMap** (Guessr's map tiles) — [privacy policy](https://osmfoundation.org/wiki/Privacy_Policy)
- **[unpkg](https://unpkg.com)** (a public CDN serving one library Guessr uses,
  delivered over Cloudflare's network)

### Children

This site and stream are intended for a general audience and are not directed at
children under 13. No information is knowingly collected from children.

